# Proxy the API through this site's own origin instead of having the browser
# talk to api.skieta.com directly. Netlify fetches from Azure server-side, so
# the client never resolves or connects to the api subdomain — which also
# keeps the app working from skieta.netlify.app while security vendors still
# have the young skieta.com domain (and its subdomains) flagged by reputation.
# Same-origin means no CORS preflight either.
# Order matters here: first match wins, so this must precede the SPA fallback.
/api/*         https://api.skieta.com/api/:splat    200
/sitemap.xml   https://api.skieta.com/sitemap.xml   200

# No rule for /og/* on purpose. A `/og/* -> /og-image.png 200` fallback used to
# sit here, and it was the only structural difference between /og-image.png,
# which Facebook processed fine, and the per-article cards under /og/, which it
# rejected as "Corrupted Image" whatever the format - a rewrite rule puts the
# path through Netlify's rewrite pipeline rather than serving it straight off
# the edge, and the cards are the only images that went through it.
#
# The cost of dropping it: a card that hasn't been generated yet now falls to
# the SPA rule below and returns index.html, so an article published without
# running scripts/generate_og_images.py previews with no image at all rather
# than the site's. Regenerate after adding an article and that never happens.

# SPA fallback — every other path is handled by the client-side router.
/*        /index.html                          200
